WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:08.340
A sympathetic neuron collects its survival signal at an axon tip centimeters from

2
00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:13.960
its nucleus. Why can't that signal diffuse home, and how does the cell move it

3
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:19.900
instead? Its axon can be a thousand times longer than its cell body is wide.

4
00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:26.230
Diffusion time equals distance squared divided by twice the diffusion constant.

5
00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:33.530
For a protein in cytoplasm, twice that constant is about 20 µm²/s.

6
00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:42.590
Across a 30 µm cell body, 30² squared is 900, and 900 ÷ 20 is 45 seconds.

7
00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:45.250
Diffusion works fine there.

8
00:00:46.283 --> 00:00:51.373
Now stretch that trip to 1 cm, or 10,000 µm.

9
00:00:51.373 --> 00:00:55.063
10,000² is 100,000,000.

10
00:00:55.063 --> 00:01:00.563
100,000,000 ÷ 20 is 5,000,000 seconds, about 58 days.

11
00:01:01.843 --> 00:01:05.163
A neuron waiting on survival genes cannot wait two months.

12
00:01:06.283 --> 00:01:07.983
Yet the message does arrive.

13
00:01:09.343 --> 00:01:13.823
Place a sealed barrier between cell bodies and axon tips, keeping their fluids

14
00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:21.103
apart. Give nerve growth factor, or NGF, only to the far axons, and many cell bodies

15
00:01:21.103 --> 00:01:25.943
survive. Inside the nucleus, the gene switch CREB turns on.

16
00:01:26.967 --> 00:01:31.467
So the signal travels inside the axon, backward toward the cell body.

17
00:01:32.727 --> 00:01:35.087
That backward trip is retrograde signaling.

18
00:01:36.407 --> 00:01:42.107
At the tip, NGF binds its receptor, TrkA, and the membrane pinches inward by

19
00:01:42.107 --> 00:01:49.347
endocytosis. Predict: once that vesicle closes, does TrkA's active kinase face into

20
00:01:49.347 --> 00:01:50.347
the vesicle or out?

21
00:01:56.300 --> 00:02:01.940
It faces out, into the cytoplasm, because the membrane folds inward like a pocket.

22
00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:07.960
That surprises students, since endocytosis sounds like swallowing everything whole.

23
00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:12.140
The side that faced outside now lines the vesicle.

24
00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:16.680
NGF stays trapped inside, still gripping TrkA.

25
00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.740
Its kinase stays in the cytoplasm, ready to phosphorylate partners.

26
00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:25.033
Now it needs a road and an engine.

27
00:02:26.473 --> 00:02:31.673
Axonal microtubules are polarized tracks, with plus ends toward the tip and minus

28
00:02:31.673 --> 00:02:33.213
ends toward the cell body.

29
00:02:34.813 --> 00:02:37.073
Cargo kinesins walk outward toward plus ends.

30
00:02:38.593 --> 00:02:43.813
Cytoplasmic dynein, aided by dynactin, walks toward minus ends, hauling the

31
00:02:43.813 --> 00:02:45.213
signaling endosome home.

32
00:02:47.217 --> 00:02:51.757
Motor transport grows linearly: time is distance divided by speed.

33
00:02:53.077 --> 00:03:00.697
At about 1 µm/s, dynein covers 10,000 µm in 10,000 seconds, under three hours.

34
00:03:02.057 --> 00:03:06.457
5,000,000 ÷ 10,000: the motor is 500 times faster.

35
00:03:07.697 --> 00:03:11.537
In the leading model, arriving endosomes trigger survival genes.

36
00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:16.760
When an axon spans centimeters, passive spreading takes months.

37
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.420
By hauling active signaling endosomes along microtubules, dynein delivers the

38
00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:25.020
survival message in hours.

39
00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:31.100
In the leading model, that is how neurons beat axonal distance, though other

40
00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.760
retrograde routes remain open.
